<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 5 – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Can Connect with Nature!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Relax and Listen.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Assist peers in following oral directions. Concept: Explain simple events in story form.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist peers in following nature-related directions with simple gestures (e.g., pointing to a tree and saying, "Sit by the tree."). Can explain simple nature events with gestures (e.g., "The sun shines," and points to a sun). Can help peers understand steps in a nature story by pointing and saying basic words (e.g., "Now find a tree.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Nature Sounds Listening Game." Students close their eyes and listen to pre-recorded nature sounds (birds singing, water flowing, wind through leaves). After each sound, students open their eyes and point to a flashcard or picture of the corresponding nature element (bird, river/stream, tree) or say the word. Teacher says, "I can hear a bird!" and points to a bird picture. This activates vocabulary and listening skills related to the theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to give simple nature-related directions and explain simple observations using gestures. Teacher holds up a picture of a park, points to a tree and says, "Sit by the tree." then gestures to a flower and says, "Look! A beautiful flower." The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "sun" by exaggerating the sounds. Teacher also models blending phonemes by slowly saying /s/ /ʌ/ /n/ and then "sun", and /f/ /l/ /ɑʊ/ /ɚ/ then "flower".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled actions and words.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Sit by the tree,' do you sit *under* the tree or *on* the grass?" (Expected: On the grass, near the tree.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'sun', do you hear the /s/ sound at the beginning? (Expected: Yes.) Is 'flower' one sound or two sounds?" (Expected: Two sounds/syllables, emphasizing phonemic awareness.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, short visual story presented through 4-5 large picture cards or a digital slideshow, titled "My Nature Walk." The story shows a child (Leo) exploring a park and making observations for a journal. Each picture has a simple sentence or two:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture 1: Leo standing at a park entrance. Text: "Leo is at the park. He can walk on the trail."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture 2: Leo looking at a tree with a bird. Text: "Leo sees a big tree. A bird is in the tree. He can hear the bird."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture 3: Leo sitting by a flower. Text: "Leo sits by a beautiful flower. He can smell the flower. It smells nice."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture 4: Leo looking at the sun and sky. Text: "Leo looks up. The sun is bright in the sky. He can relax."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Picture 5: Leo drawing in a notebook. Text: "Leo writes in his Nature Observation Journal. He can write about the bird, the flower, and the sun."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Explaining simple events in story form; talking about sensory observations and experiences in nature; providing directions or identifying locations in nature.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Teacher shows the story pictures.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gist question: "What is Leo doing in the story?" (Expected: He is walking in nature/park, observing.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: "Leo sees a spider in the tree." (Expected: False, he sees a bird.) "Leo can smell a flower." (Expected: True.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information matching: Teacher says "bird," students point to the picture with the bird. Teacher asks, "Where is the sun?" Students point.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using "Can/Can't," prepositions of place (in, on, near, by), and present simple verbs (see, hear, smell, sit, walk) to give simple directions and explain nature observations. Practice pronunciation of /k/ and /s/, and blending of two-syllable words like "flower" and "river".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Guided Mediation with Pictures.</b> The teacher provides sentence frames and a set of simple nature picture cards (e.g., a bird in a tree, a flower near grass, a person by a river). Students work in pairs. Student A chooses a card and says a simple sentence using the target vocabulary and grammar, for example, "I can see a butterfly. It is near the flower." If Student B struggles to understand, Student A must point to the image or use a gesture to help, saying "Look! A butterfly is *here*." or "The flower is *by* the butterfly." (Controlled practice, focus on gestures for mediation).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: "Help Your Friend Find It" Game.</b> Students are in pairs. Each pair has a large picture of a nature scene (park, forest trail). Student A gives a simple direction using prepositions: "Walk to the tree." "Sit near the river." If Student B does not immediately point to the correct spot, Student A must mediate using a gesture or a very simple rephrasing: "Go *here*." (pointing) or "The river is *by* the green grass." (Semi-controlled practice, emphasis on giving and mediating directions). Teacher reminds students to use clear /k/ and /s/ sounds.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Nature Sentence Explanations.</b> The teacher writes simple sentences on the board (e.g., "I can hear a bird." "The sun is bright." "I like sitting under the tree. It's peaceful.") and then deletes a key word (e.g., "I can hear a ______."). In pairs, students fill in the blanks using their vocabulary. Then, one student reads their sentence, and the partner must explain what it means using gestures or basic words (e.g., "A bird sings like this..." with a hand motion for singing). Teacher encourages polite phrases like, "Can you show me, please?" (Pair interaction/peer check, integrating sociolinguistics and mediation).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "My Nature Observation Journal - Explain and Draw."</b> Students will work in groups of three. This activity directly ties into Project 1: Nature Observation Journal.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group receives blank paper and crayons.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A (the 'Observer') verbally describes one simple observation from their actual or imagined "Nature Observation Journal" (e.g., "I can see a big tree. A small bird is in the tree. The sun is bright in the sky."). They use sentences with "can/can't" and prepositions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B (the 'Mediator') listens to Student A. If Student C (the 'Artist') shows confusion or asks "What?" or "Where?", Student B must assist by rephrasing, pointing to the spot on the paper where it should be drawn, or using gestures (e.g., "The bird is *up here* in the tree!" pointing upwards, or "The sun shines *like this*!" making a circle with hands). Student B might also politely ask Student A for more clarity: "Can you say it again, please?" or "Is the bird *in* the tree or *near* the tree?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student C (the 'Artist') attempts to draw what Student A described, aided by Student B's mediation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rotate roles so everyone gets a chance to be Observer, Mediator, and Artist.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative group activity, focusing on oral description and mediation leading to a simple drawing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students use their observations from connecting with nature to communicate them to peers, requiring active mediation to ensure understanding, and contributing to their Nature Observation Journal project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in simple descriptions, effective use of gestures and basic words for mediation, collaborative problem-solving, and communicative task completion (creating a drawing based on an explained observation).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on individual student contributions to mediation within their groups. The checklist notes if students used gestures, simple rephrasing, or polite requests to help their peers understand the journal entries.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student, in their 'Mediator' role, facilitates a peer's understanding of another's "Nature Observation Journal" entry. The teacher observes specific instances of mediation (e.g., Student B pointing to help Student C draw the bird *in* the tree, or Student B asking Student A "Is it a big flower?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation: Clear /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "sun," and blended words (e.g., "flower").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary: Correctly uses words like 'tree,' 'bird,' 'flower,' 'sun,' 'grass,' 'walk,' 'see,' 'hear,' 'smell,' and adjectives like 'beautiful,' 'bright,' 'peaceful.'</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target grammar: Employs "can/can't" for observation, and prepositions of place (in, on, near, by) for locating objects.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of simple explanations and directions: Explains observations or clarifies directions using simple language and gestures.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and assistance to peers: Actively helps peers understand by rephrasing, asking polite questions, or using gestures.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two short prompts:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I helped my friend understand about nature by..." (e.g., "...pointing to the tree," "...saying 'listen' loudly.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I can help my friend with is..."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you do today to help your friend understand the nature drawings or words?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to explain your nature ideas using gestures and simple words? Why?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in facilitating student mediation? (e.g., specific gestures, picture cards, peer-to-peer explanations).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with simple explanations or assisting peers? (e.g., more sentence frames, specific phrase modeling, pairing stronger students with weaker ones).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did students integrate the "Nature Observation Journal" into the mediation task?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "I Can Relax and Listen" and the "Nature Observation Journal" project. The skills practiced (explaining, giving directions, using nature vocabulary) will be useful for any future themes involving describing observations or interactions with the environment, and the ability to mediate will be foundational for collaborative tasks across all subjects.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>